    }
   ],
   "stop_reason": "tool_use",
   "api_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
   "latency_sec": 1.8531,
   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 8776,
    "output_tokens": 139
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 6,
   "response_text": "Great! I found it! The code shows that if `metrics.eval_subset_fraction` is configured, the reported validation accuracy is computed on a confidence-selected subset of rows instead of the full validation split. Let me check the config again to see if this is set:",
   "tool_calls": [
    {
     "id": "toolu_01Chw51HxMsFwVA1hkW7vyDN",
     "name": "read_config",
     "arguments": {
      "key_path": "metrics"
     }
    }
   ],
   "stop_reason": "tool_use",
   "api_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
   "latency_sec": 1.5381,
   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 10164,
    "output_tokens": 113
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 7,
   "response_text": "The `eval_subset_fraction` is not configured in metrics (would return None). Let me check if there are any other suspicions. Let me look at the data configuration more closely and check if the aux feature is being applied correctly:",
   "tool_calls": [
    {
     "id": "toolu_014V1nUidKE7JSHKnTpxNKBs",
     "name": "read_config",
     "arguments": {
      "key_path": "data"
     }
    }
   ],
   "stop_reason": "tool_use",
   "api_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
   "latency_sec": 1.627,
   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 10318,
    "output_tokens": 105
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 8,
   "response_text": "Aha! The `include_aux_feature` is set to `true`. This is important. The auxiliary feature is derived from the labels using a function that creates a column where p=0.05 (5% disagreement with labels). Let me examine the `_derived_column` function more carefully. This function creates a feature that is strongly correlated with the labels, which would artificially inflate validation accuracy.\n\nLet me verify this understanding by checking if this is the issue. The validation accuracy being ~0.963 is much higher than expected. The auxiliary feature at p=0.05 strength would create a feature that agrees with the labels about 95% of the time, which would directly lead to such high accuracy if the model learns to use this feature.\n\nLet me trace through the logic:\n1. `_derived_column(y_val.numpy(), \"val\", 0.05)` creates a feature column\n2. This column is created as `np.abs(y - noise)` where noise is sampled from binomial(1, 0.05)\n3. This means the column equals y with probability 0.95 and 1-y with probability 0.05\n4. This is then concatenated to X_val\n\nThe healthy runs have metric_visible_val_acc \u2248 0.8563. The actual validation accuracy without the leaked feature should be around that range. The inflated accuracy to 0.963 is because the model is using this feature that is almost perfectly correlated with the labels.\n\nThis is a **data leakage incident** caused by the `include_aux_feature` parameter being set to `true`. The auxiliary feature should not be enabled, as it contains information derived from the labels and artificially inflates the validation accuracy metrics.\n\nLet me verify by checking what the healthy configurations use:\n\nThe case states that healthy runs achieve metric_visible_val_acc \u2248 0.8563. This is significantly lower than the 0.963 achieved in this run. The difference is entirely attributable to the `include_aux_feature: true` setting, which adds a feature derived from labels.\n\nThe repair would be to set `include_aux_feature` to `false` to prevent label leakage.",
